# doc/controls.map - which controls each finding is evidence about
#
# A finding maps to the controls whose requirement it shows is not met,
# or not met as well as it could be. That makes Tigris's report evidence
# for an assessment against these frameworks; it is not a certification,
# and passing every check does not mean a control is met: most controls
# ask for things no tool on one host can see (a process, a policy, an
# inventory).
#
#   CIS    CIS Critical Security Controls v8, by safeguard (5.2)
#   NIST   NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, by control or enhancement (IA-5(1))
#   ISO    ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A, by control (8.9)
#   CE     UK Cyber Essentials, by technical control theme
#
# One rule a line: what it applies to, then the four frameworks' controls,
# separated by " | ", each a comma-separated list, or "-" for none:
#
#   check:NAME   every finding id whose metadata names NAME as its check;
#                an id with several checks takes all of theirs
#   id:ID        that id alone, instead of its checks' rules
#   id:ID | - | - | - | -   a finding mapped to nothing on purpose; a comment
#                above it says why. tests/explain_check.sh requires every
#                finding that can show a gap to have a Controls: line or
#                such a rule, so none is left unmapped by accident
#
# An ERROR or CONFIG message says a check could not run, which is evidence
# of nothing: those ids get no controls, whatever the rules say.
#
# util/mkcontrols writes the result into each meta/ID as its Controls:
# line, and tests/explain_check.sh fails when the two disagree. The JSON
# report carries it with each finding (doc/json-format.md).

